<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">(c) </span>All-in-one: <math id="S1.F1.sf3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;" mathcolor="#FF0000">1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S1.F1.sf3.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;"> access</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Illustration of multi-class classification strategies for <math id="S1.F1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> classes. The individual sample (<math id="S1.F1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\star" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⋆</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\star</annotation></semantics></math>) is queried repeatedly in (a) and (b), but only once in (c). Each color represents a class.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Backgrounds</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Differential Privacy</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Differential privacy (DP) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> establishes a mathematical framework to ensure the privacy of training data caused by changes in an individual sample, such as deletion and modification. To prevent the leakage of individual information through query responses, its formal definition is as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 1</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Differential privacy) A randomized mechanism <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{(\epsilon,\delta)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold" stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">ϵ</mi><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">,</mo><mi>𝛅</mi><mo class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{(\epsilon,\delta)}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmdefinition1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">-differential privacy (<math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{(\epsilon,\delta)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold" mathvariant="bold">ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝛅</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{(\epsilon,\delta)}</annotation></semantics></math>-DP)</span> if, for two neighboring datasets <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D,D^{\prime}\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D,D^{\prime}\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, which differ in exactly one data sample, and for any set of possible outputs <math id="Thmdefinition1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}\subseteq Range(\mathcal{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}\subseteq Range(\mathcal{M})</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Pr[\mathcal{M}(D)\in\mathcal{O}]\leq e^{\epsilon}Pr[\mathcal{M}(D^{\prime})\in\mathcal{O}]+\delta." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msup><mi>P</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Pr[\mathcal{M}(D)\in\mathcal{O}]\leq e^{\epsilon}Pr[\mathcal{M}(D^{\prime})\in\mathcal{O}]+\delta.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The privacy loss is quantified by the parameter <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>, where smaller values of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> indicate a stronger privacy guarantee. The parameter <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> represents the probability of failure for the mechanism <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 2</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<math id="Thmdefinition2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> Sensitivity)<span id="Thmdefinition2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For a function <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:{\mathcal{D}}\to\mathbb{R}^{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:{\mathcal{D}}\to\mathbb{R}^{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, the <em id="Thmdefinition2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">sensitivity</em> <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as</span></p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{f}=\max_{D,D^{\prime}}\|f(D)-f(D^{\prime})\|_{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{f}=\max_{D,D^{\prime}}\|f(D)-f(D^{\prime})\|_{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmdefinition2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmdefinition2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmdefinition2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> differ by at most one element.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce widely used properties as remarks for DP, i.e., composition to boost the sequential application, and post-processing to preserve the privacy guarantee of outputs that are already private.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1</span></span><span id="Thmremark1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Composition <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>)
Let <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}:\mathcal{X}\rightarrow\mathcal{R}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}:\mathcal{X}\rightarrow\mathcal{R}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> be an <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon_{1},\delta_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon_{1},\delta_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>-DP algorithm, and let <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2}:\mathcal{X}\rightarrow\mathcal{R}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2}:\mathcal{X}\rightarrow\mathcal{R}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> be an <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon_{2},\delta_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon_{2},\delta_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>-DP algorithm. Then, their combination <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1,2}:\mathcal{X}\rightarrow\mathcal{R}_{1}\times\mathcal{R}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1,2}:\mathcal{X}\rightarrow\mathcal{R}_{1}\times\mathcal{R}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> by the mapping: <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1,2}(\cdot)=(\mathcal{M}_{1}(\cdot),\mathcal{M}_{2}(\cdot))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1,2}(\cdot)=(\mathcal{M}_{1}(\cdot),\mathcal{M}_{2}(\cdot))</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon_{1}+\epsilon_{2},\delta_{1}+\delta_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon_{1}+\epsilon_{2},\delta_{1}+\delta_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>-DP.
For <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, the composition of <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> algorithms, where each algorithm meets <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon,\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon,\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>, satisfies</span></p>
<table id="S2.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Pr[\mathcal{M}(D)\in\mathcal{O}]\leq e^{k\epsilon}Pr[\mathcal{M}(D^{\prime})\in\mathcal{O}]+k\delta." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi></mrow></msup><mi>P</mi><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Pr[\mathcal{M}(D)\in\mathcal{O}]\leq e^{k\epsilon}Pr[\mathcal{M}(D^{\prime})\in\mathcal{O}]+k\delta.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2</span></span><span id="Thmremark2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Post-processing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>)
If a mechanism <math id="Thmremark2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}:\mathcal{X}\rightarrow\mathcal{R}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}:\mathcal{X}\rightarrow\mathcal{R}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Thmremark2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon,\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon,\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>-DP, for any randomized mapping <math id="Thmremark2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h:\mathcal{R}_{1}\rightarrow\mathcal{R}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h:\mathcal{R}_{1}\rightarrow\mathcal{R}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmremark2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\circ\mathcal{M}:\mathcal{X}\rightarrow\mathcal{R}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\circ\mathcal{M}:\mathcal{X}\rightarrow\mathcal{R}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is at least <math id="Thmremark2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon,\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon,\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>-DP.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Balle and Wang [18]</a></cite> proposed an analytic Gaussian mechanism to reduce the noise level of DP:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 3</span></span><span id="Thmremark3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Analytic Gaussian Mechanism <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>) Let <math id="Thmremark3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> be a function with <math id="Thmremark3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> sensitivity <math id="Thmremark3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>.
A Gaussian output perturbation mechanism <math id="Thmremark3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}({\mathbf{x}})=f({\mathbf{x}})+{\mathbf{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>𝐳</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}({\mathbf{x}})=f({\mathbf{x}})+{\mathbf{z}}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmremark3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{z}}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^{2}\mathbf{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{z}}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma^{2}\mathbf{I})</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies <math id="Thmremark3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon,\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon,\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>-DP for all <math id="Thmremark3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, the if and only if</span></p>
<table id="S2.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi\!\left(\frac{\Delta}{2\sigma}-\frac{\epsilon\sigma}{\Delta}\right)-e^{\epsilon}\,\Phi\!\left(-\frac{\Delta}{2\sigma}-\frac{\epsilon\sigma}{\Delta}\right)\leq\delta," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.497em;" width="0.497em"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>ϵ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mpadded style="width:0.497em;" width="0.497em"><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi></mpadded><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi\!\left(\frac{\Delta}{2\sigma}-\frac{\epsilon\sigma}{\Delta}\right)-e^{\epsilon}\,\Phi\!\left(-\frac{\Delta}{2\sigma}-\frac{\epsilon\sigma}{\Delta}\right)\leq\delta,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmremark3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi</annotation></semantics></math> is the cumulative distribution function of the standard normal distribution.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Multi-class Support Vector Machine</h3>

<section id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Binary Support Vector Machine </h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Support Vector Machines (SVMs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> are a broadly used machine learning method with margin maximization for building binary classification boundaries.
Consider a training dataset with <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> samples, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}=\{{\mathbf{x}}_{i},y_{i}\}_{i=1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}=\{{\mathbf{x}}_{i},y_{i}\}_{i=1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, where each <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is a feature vector and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{i}\in\{-1,+1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{i}\in\{-1,+1\}</annotation></semantics></math> its corresponding label.
Then, the objective of SVM is as follows:</p>
<table id="S2.E5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S2.E5X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E5X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\min_{{\mathbf{w}},b}\frac{1}{2}\|{\mathbf{w}}\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>𝐰</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min_{{\mathbf{w}},b}\frac{1}{2}\|{\mathbf{w}}\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E5X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+\frac{C}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\xi_{i}\quad\text{s.t.}\quad y_{i}({\mathbf{w}}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}_{i}+b)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mi>ξ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐰</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\frac{C}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\xi_{i}\quad\text{s.t.}\quad y_{i}({\mathbf{w}}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}_{i}+b)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E5X.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\geq 1-\xi_{i},\quad\xi_{i}\geq 0,\forall i," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ξ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\geq 1-\xi_{i},\quad\xi_{i}\geq 0,\forall i,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{w}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{w}}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is the weight vector, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math> is the bias term, <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the slack variables accounting for misclassifications, and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is the regularization parameter controlling the trade-off between margin and errors. The Wolfe dual of Equation <a href="#S2.E5" title="In Binary Support Vector Machine ‣ 2.2 Multi-class Support Vector Machine ‣ 2 Backgrounds ‣ Multi-Class Support Vector Machine with Differential Privacy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> is formulated as follows:</p>
<table id="S2.E6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S2.E6X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E6X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\max_{{\bm{\alpha}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>𝜶</mi></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\max_{{\bm{\alpha}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E6X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^{n}\alpha_{i}-\frac{1}{2}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{j=1}^{n}\alpha_{i}\alpha_{j}y_{i}y_{j}{\mathbf{x}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}_{j}\quad\text{s.t.}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>s.t.</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sum_{i=1}^{n}\alpha_{i}-\frac{1}{2}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{j=1}^{n}\alpha_{i}\alpha_{j}y_{i}y_{j}{\mathbf{x}}_{i}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}_{j}\quad\text{s.t.}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E6X.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle 0\leq\alpha_{i}\leq\frac{C}{n},\quad\sum_{i=1}^{n}\alpha_{i}y_{i}=0,\quad\forall i," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>α</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mi>α</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle 0\leq\alpha_{i}\leq\frac{C}{n},\quad\sum_{i=1}^{n}\alpha_{i}y_{i}=0,\quad\forall i,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.5" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\bm{\alpha}}=\{\alpha_{1},\ldots,\alpha_{n}\}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜶</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\bm{\alpha}}=\{\alpha_{1},\ldots,\alpha_{n}\}\in\mathbb{R}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> are the dual variables. Utilizing the Karush-Kuhn-Tucker (KKT), we can obtain the optimal parameter with <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{{\mathbf{w}}}=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\tilde{\alpha}_{i}y_{i}{\mathbf{x}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{{\mathbf{w}}}=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\tilde{\alpha}_{i}y_{i}{\mathbf{x}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\alpha}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\alpha}</annotation></semantics></math> are the optimal dual parameters of the convex optimization (<a href="#S2.E6" title="In Binary Support Vector Machine ‣ 2.2 Multi-class Support Vector Machine ‣ 2 Backgrounds ‣ Multi-Class Support Vector Machine with Differential Privacy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>). Then, the binary support function is formulated as <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f({\mathbf{x}})=\tilde{{\mathbf{w}}}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}+b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f({\mathbf{x}})=\tilde{{\mathbf{w}}}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}+b</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Multi-Class Support Vector Machine</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The most common way to expand binary SVM to multi-class is to use one-versus-one (OvR) or one-versus-one (OvO) approaches by training each classifier for each class pair or each class versus the rest, respectively. After training multiple binary classification models, we can make a decision
<math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{y}=\arg\max_{k\in[c]}\bigl\{{\mathbf{w}}_{k}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}+b_{k}\bigr\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{y}=\arg\max_{k\in[c]}\bigl\{{\mathbf{w}}_{k}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}+b_{k}\bigr\}</annotation></semantics></math>
for <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> classes and class-wise weights <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{w}}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{w}}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and bias <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Stacking the class–wise weights gives a weight matrix
<math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W=[{\mathbf{w}}_{1},\dots,{\mathbf{w}}_{c}]\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W=[{\mathbf{w}}_{1},\dots,{\mathbf{w}}_{c}]\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times c}</annotation></semantics></math> and biases
<math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{b}}\in\mathbb{R}^{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐛</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{b}}\in\mathbb{R}^{c}</annotation></semantics></math> for multi-class classification.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Instead of calculating support vectors for each binary classification, a line of work investigated training multi-class SVM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> at once, which is called all-in-one SVM methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>. Each all-in-one method has its own design for defining the margin. Among them, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Weston and Watkins [15]</a></cite> (WW-SVM) formulated a multi–class classification as a single joint optimization problem:</p>
<table id="S2.E7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S2.E7X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E7X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\min_{W,{\mathbf{b}}}\quad\frac{1}{2}\sum_{k=1}^{c}\|\mathbf{w}_{k}\|_{2}^{2}+\frac{C}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{k\neq y_{i}}\xi_{ki}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐛</mi></mrow></munder><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>c</mi></munderover></mstyle><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></munder></mstyle><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min_{W,{\mathbf{b}}}\quad\frac{1}{2}\sum_{k=1}^{c}\|\mathbf{w}_{k}\|_{2}^{2}+\frac{C}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{k\neq y_{i}}\xi_{ki}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.E7Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E7Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{s.t.}\quad{\mathbf{w}}_{y_{i}}^{\!\top}{\mathbf{x}}_{i}+b_{y_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{s.t.}\quad{\mathbf{w}}_{y_{i}}^{\!\top}{\mathbf{x}}_{i}+b_{y_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E7Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\geq{\mathbf{w}}_{k}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}_{i}+b_{k}+1-\xi_{ki},\xi_{ki}\geq 0,k\in[c],k\neq y_{i},i\in[n]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>𝐰</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>b</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ξ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>y</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\geq{\mathbf{w}}_{k}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}_{i}+b_{k}+1-\xi_{ki},\xi_{ki}\geq 0,k\in[c],k\neq y_{i},i\in[n].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Crammer and Singer (CS-SVM) proposed to penalize only the largest violating class, not all pairs, per sample <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>.
Then the optimization can be written as follows:</p>
<table id="S2.E8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S2.E8X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E8X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\min_{W,{\mathbf{b}}}\quad\frac{1}{2}\sum_{k=1}^{c}\|\mathbf{w}_{k}\|_{2}^{2}+\frac{C}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\xi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐛</mi></mrow></munder><mspace style="width:1.167em;" width="1.167em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>c</mi></munderover></mstyle><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min_{W,{\mathbf{b}}}\quad\frac{1}{2}\sum_{k=1}^{c}\|\mathbf{w}_{k}\|_{2}^{2}+\frac{C}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\xi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.E8Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E8Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{s.t.}\quad{\mathbf{w}}_{y_{i}}^{\!\top}{\mathbf{x}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{s.t.}\quad{\mathbf{w}}_{y_{i}}^{\!\top}{\mathbf{x}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E8Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle-{\mathbf{w}}_{k}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}_{i}+\upsilon_{y_{i},k}\geq 1-\xi_{i},\xi_{i}\geq 0,k\in[c],i\in[n]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐰</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>υ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ξ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>ξ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle-{\mathbf{w}}_{k}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}_{i}+\upsilon_{y_{i},k}\geq 1-\xi_{i},\xi_{i}\geq 0,k\in[c],i\in[n].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\upsilon_{y_{i},k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>υ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\upsilon_{y_{i},k}</annotation></semantics></math> equals to 1 if <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=y_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=y_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and 0 otherwise.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">More recently, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nie et al. [17]</a></cite> developed a concept of maximizing minimum margin SVM (M<sup id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.2.1" class="ltx_sup">3</sup>-SVM), not just maximizing the margin between class pairs. With the support function <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{kl}({\mathbf{x}})=({\mathbf{w}}_{k}-{\mathbf{w}}_{l})^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}+b_{k}-b_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{kl}({\mathbf{x}})=({\mathbf{w}}_{k}-{\mathbf{w}}_{l})^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}+b_{k}-b_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> between class <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k&lt;l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k&lt;l</annotation></semantics></math>, its objective function with <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-norm is</p>
<table id="S2.E9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S2.E9X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E9X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\min_{W,\mathbf{b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐛</mi></mrow></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min_{W,\mathbf{b}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E9X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{1}{2}\sum_{k&lt;l}\|\mathbf{w}_{k}-\mathbf{w}_{l}\|_{2}^{2}+\frac{C}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{k&lt;l}\xi_{ikl}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{1}{2}\sum_{k&lt;l}\|\mathbf{w}_{k}-\mathbf{w}_{l}\|_{2}^{2}+\frac{C}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{k&lt;l}\xi_{ikl}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.E9Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E9Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{s.t. }" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext>s.t.</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{s.t. }</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E9Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left\{\begin{aligned} f_{kl}(\mathbf{x}_{i})&amp;\geq 1-\xi_{ikl},&amp;&amp;\text{for }y_{i}=k,\\
f_{kl}(\mathbf{x}_{i})&amp;\leq-1+\xi_{ikl},&amp;&amp;\text{for }y_{i}=l,\end{aligned}\right.\quad\xi_{ikl}\geq 0,k&lt;l,i\in[n]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="0pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>for </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>for </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mi>ξ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left\{\begin{aligned} f_{kl}(\mathbf{x}_{i})&amp;\geq 1-\xi_{ikl},&amp;&amp;\text{for }y_{i}=k,\\
f_{kl}(\mathbf{x}_{i})&amp;\leq-1+\xi_{ikl},&amp;&amp;\text{for }y_{i}=l,\end{aligned}\right.\quad\xi_{ikl}\geq 0,k&lt;l,i\in[n].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, WW-SVM enforces c pair-wise constraints per sample, each with its own slack <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{ki}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{ki}</annotation></semantics></math>; CS-SVM imposes a single constraint by penalizing only the most-violating class and therefore uses a single slack <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>; whereas M<sup id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">3</span></sup>-SVM simultaneously applies all pair-wise constraints per sample, introducing a distinct slack <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{ikl}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{ikl}</annotation></semantics></math> for every class pair.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.T1" class="ltx_table">

<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Comparison of multi-class SVM strategies (<math id="S2.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>: # of classes, <math id="S2.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>: # of training samples).</figcaption><div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<div id="S2.T1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_figure_panel ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:341.6pt;height:33.4pt;vertical-align:-15.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-81.5pt,8.0pt) scale(0.67706866779909,0.67706866779909) ;">
