</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Differentially Private Multi-Class SVM</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Motivation: Advantages of All-in-One SVM for Privacy</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We begin by comparing the trade-offs of multi-class SVM strategies in Table <a href="#S2.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Multi-Class Support Vector Machine ‣ 2.2 Multi-class Support Vector Machine ‣ 2 Backgrounds ‣ Multi-Class Support Vector Machine with Differential Privacy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Each method has its own strengths. For instance, the OvO approach requires only <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2N/c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2N/c</annotation></semantics></math> dual variables per problem, which allows it to scale efficiently and be easily parallelized. The OvR method grows linearly with the number of classes, avoiding the quadratic explosion at inference time. The primary advantage of the all-in-one SVM method is its ability to build a robust classifier in one step by calculating the pair-wise or maximum margin at once, compared to an ensemble of binary classifiers that may become overfitted to each individual class <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>. Note that efficiency improvements may vary depending on the specific implementation of each algorithm.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">When focusing on privacy, we observe that all-in-one SVMs have a clear advantage. They reduce the number of data accesses needed to build classifiers. In contrast, binary classification methods such as OvO or OvR require multiple accesses to data samples, which increase linearly with the number of classes <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, consuming the privacy budget repeatedly with each access. Specifically, the composition theorem (Remark <a href="#Thmremark1" title="Remark 1. ‣ 2.1 Differential Privacy ‣ 2 Backgrounds ‣ Multi-Class Support Vector Machine with Differential Privacy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) states that the number of accesses to individual data samples directly affects the noise level if each mechanism has a dependency on training data. When applying this principle to the OvR case, the composition of c classifiers requires c<math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> privacy budget when each binary classifier requires <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, to maintain the same total privacy budget, we can only allocate <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\epsilon}{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>c</mi></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\epsilon}{c}</annotation></semantics></math> to each binary classifier, amplifying the amount of noise on each classifier.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In the following subsections, we propose differentially Private Multi-class SVM (PMSVM) with weight and gradient perturbations tailored for all-in-one SVMs that can significantly lower the privacy cost of building DP classifiers. In contrast to existing OvO or OvR methods, the proposed PMSVM requires only one data access to build a multi-class classifier, allowing us to utilize the full privacy budget <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Weight Perturbation for All-in-one PMSVM</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Motivated by the DP empirical risk minimization (ERM) methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, we first propose a weight perturbation method for PMSVM (PMSVM-WP). In DP ERM problems, we estimate the optimal weight <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{{\mathbf{w}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{{\mathbf{w}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and protect it by adding random noise proportional to the sensitivity. The sensitivity in Definition <a href="#Thmdefinition2" title="Definition 2 (𝐿_2 Sensitivity). ‣ 2.1 Differential Privacy ‣ 2 Backgrounds ‣ Multi-Class Support Vector Machine with Differential Privacy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> indicates how significantly the weight can vary with the worst-case changes to individual data points.
The Wolf dual problem of the all-in-one SVMs can be unified as follows <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>:</p>
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E10X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E10X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\min_{\bm{\alpha}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><munder><mi>min</mi><mi>𝜶</mi></munder><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min_{\bm{\alpha}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E10X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{1}{2}\sum_{i,p}\sum_{j,q}M_{y_{i},p,y_{j},q}\,\mathbf{x}_{i}^{\top}\mathbf{x}_{j}\,\alpha_{i,p}\,\alpha_{j,q}-\sum_{i,p}\alpha_{i,p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></munder></mstyle><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{1}{2}\sum_{i,p}\sum_{j,q}M_{y_{i},p,y_{j},q}\,\mathbf{x}_{i}^{\top}\mathbf{x}_{j}\,\alpha_{i,p}\,\alpha_{j,q}-\sum_{i,p}\alpha_{i,p}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E10Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E10Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{s.t. }0\leq\alpha_{i,p}\leq\frac{C}{n}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>s.t. </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{s.t. }0\leq\alpha_{i,p}\leq\frac{C}{n},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E10Xa.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\sum_{p\in P_{y_{i}}}\alpha_{i,p}\leq\frac{C}{n},\forall\,i\in[n],\quad\sum_{i=1}^{n}\!\sum_{p\in P_{y_{i}}}\alpha_{i,p}\,\nu_{y_{i},p,k}=0,\quad\forall\,k\in[c]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow></msub><mi>α</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo><mo rspace="0.337em">∀</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow></msub><mi>α</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mi>ν</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mo rspace="0.337em">∀</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\sum_{p\in P_{y_{i}}}\alpha_{i,p}\leq\frac{C}{n},\forall\,i\in[n],\quad\sum_{i=1}^{n}\!\sum_{p\in P_{y_{i}}}\alpha_{i,p}\,\nu_{y_{i},p,k}=0,\quad\forall\,k\in[c],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{y_{i}}=Y\setminus\{y_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{y_{i}}=Y\setminus\{y_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of non-true class indices for sample <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>;
<math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{y_{i},p,k}=e_{y_{i},k}-e_{p,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{y_{i},p,k}=e_{y_{i},k}-e_{p,k}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{s,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{s,k}</annotation></semantics></math> denoting the <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th component of the basis vector <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{e}}_{s}\in\mathbb{R}^{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐞</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>c</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{e}}_{s}\in\mathbb{R}^{c}</annotation></semantics></math>;
and <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{y_{i},p,y_{j},q}=\sum_{k=1}^{c}\nu_{y_{i},p,k}\,\nu_{y_{j},q,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{y_{i},p,y_{j},q}=\sum_{k=1}^{c}\nu_{y_{i},p,k}\,\nu_{y_{j},q,k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The convexity of convex quadratic optimization problems remains in the dual formulation of all-in-one SVMs. Therefore, we take a closer look at the leave-one-out method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite> of support vector classifiers, which bounds the difference of the support function after changing one individual sample. To calculate the sensitivity of optimal weights and support functions, we need to track the sensitivity in the dual function since the dual variables of SVM are defined per data sample.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Weight Perturbation)<span id="Thmdefinition3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{w}}=\tilde{\mathbf{w}}+{\mathbf{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mi>𝐳</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{w}}=\tilde{\mathbf{w}}+{\mathbf{z}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmdefinition3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, where <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{z}}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_{\mathbf{w}}^{2}\mathbf{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>𝐰</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{z}}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_{\mathbf{w}}^{2}\mathbf{I})</annotation></semantics></math> for optimal weight <math id="Thmdefinition3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbf{w}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbf{w}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To calculate the sensitivity of <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbf{w}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbf{w}}</annotation></semantics></math> for DP, we derive a new Lemma, a multi-class extension of the leave-one-out bound of SVM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>, as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemma1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlemma1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 1</span></span><span id="Thmlemma1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlemma1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlemma1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For a convex function <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, a dataset <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, and input scaler <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>, let <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{{\mathbf{w}}}_{D}=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\tilde{\alpha}_{i}g({\mathbf{x}}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>D</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{{\mathbf{w}}}_{D}=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\tilde{\alpha}_{i}g({\mathbf{x}}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="Thmlemma1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\tilde{\alpha}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{\alpha}_{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\tilde{\alpha}_{1},\ldots,\tilde{\alpha}_{n})</annotation></semantics></math> is the solution to:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemma1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{\bm{\alpha}}\left(\frac{1}{2}\sum_{i,p}\sum_{j,q}\sum_{k}\alpha_{i,p}\alpha_{j,q}\nu_{y_{i},p}\nu_{y_{j},q}g({\mathbf{x}}_{i})^{T}g({\mathbf{x}}_{j})+\sum_{i,p}T(-\alpha_{i,p})\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mi>𝜶</mi></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>k</mi></munder><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>T</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{\bm{\alpha}}\left(\frac{1}{2}\sum_{i,p}\sum_{j,q}\sum_{k}\alpha_{i,p}\alpha_{j,q}\nu_{y_{i},p}\nu_{y_{j},q}g({\mathbf{x}}_{i})^{T}g({\mathbf{x}}_{j})+\sum_{i,p}T(-\alpha_{i,p})\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemma1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlemma1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmlemma1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be <math id="Thmlemma1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> with the <math id="Thmlemma1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-th point <math id="Thmlemma1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> removed, and let <math id="Thmlemma1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{{\mathbf{w}}}_{D^{n}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msup><mi>D</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{{\mathbf{w}}}_{D^{n}}</annotation></semantics></math> be defined similarly. Then the difference of the weights between original and leave-one-out SVMs is bounded as:</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{k=1}^{c}||{\mathbf{w}}_{k}^{[n]}-{\mathbf{w}}_{k}||^{2}\leq\lambda_{\max}(G)\|\tilde{\alpha}_{n}\|^{2}||g({\mathbf{x}}_{n})||^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>c</mi></munderover><msup><mrow><mo lspace="0em" stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>α</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{k=1}^{c}||{\mathbf{w}}_{k}^{[n]}-{\mathbf{w}}_{k}||^{2}\leq\lambda_{\max}(G)\|\tilde{\alpha}_{n}\|^{2}||g({\mathbf{x}}_{n})||^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Using this Lemma, we can calculate the sensitivity of the weights <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{{\mathbf{w}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{{\mathbf{w}}}</annotation></semantics></math> of all-in-one SVMs.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(DP guarantee of weight perturbation)<span id="Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{w}}=\tilde{\mathbf{w}}+{\mathbf{z}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mi>𝐳</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{w}}=\tilde{\mathbf{w}}+{\mathbf{z}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> (Definition <a href="#Thmdefinition3" title="Definition 3 (Weight Perturbation). ‣ 3.2 Weight Perturbation for All-in-one PMSVM ‣ 3 Differentially Private Multi-Class SVM ‣ Multi-Class Support Vector Machine with Differential Privacy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) satisfies an <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon,\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon,\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>-DP when <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{z}}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_{\mathbf{w}}^{2}\mathbf{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><mi>𝐰</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{z}}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_{\mathbf{w}}^{2}\mathbf{I})</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\mathbf{w}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>𝐰</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\mathbf{w}}</annotation></semantics></math> in Remark <a href="#S2.E4" title="In Remark 3. ‣ 2.1 Differential Privacy ‣ 2 Backgrounds ‣ Multi-Class Support Vector Machine with Differential Privacy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, the sensitivity of the all-in-one SVM weight <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\mathbf{w}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>𝐰</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\mathbf{w}}</annotation></semantics></math> is:</span></p>
<table id="S3.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\mathbf{w}}=\frac{2C}{n}\,\sqrt{\lambda_{\max}(G)},\qquad G_{pq}=\langle\nu_{y,p},\nu_{y,q}\rangle," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>𝐰</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\mathbf{w}}=\frac{2C}{n}\,\sqrt{\lambda_{\max}(G)},\qquad G_{pq}=\langle\nu_{y,p},\nu_{y,q}\rangle,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> is the largest eigenvalue of the Gram matrix <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.
The support function
<math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{f}(\mathbf{x})=\arg\max_{k\in[c]}\bigl\{\tilde{\mathbf{w}}_{k}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\bigr\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>max</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>k</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{f}(\mathbf{x})=\arg\max_{k\in[c]}\bigl\{\tilde{\mathbf{w}}_{k}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}\bigr\}</annotation></semantics></math> is also <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon,\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon,\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>-DP.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Detailed proofs of Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma1" title="Lemma 1. ‣ 3.2 Weight Perturbation for All-in-one PMSVM ‣ 3 Differentially Private Multi-Class SVM ‣ Multi-Class Support Vector Machine with Differential Privacy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1 (DP guarantee of weight perturbation). ‣ 3.2 Weight Perturbation for All-in-one PMSVM ‣ 3 Differentially Private Multi-Class SVM ‣ Multi-Class Support Vector Machine with Differential Privacy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> are provided in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Proofs ‣ Multi-Class Support Vector Machine with Differential Privacy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>. However, this is a generalized version of the weight perturbation in a binary setting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>. We can obtain the same sensitivity of binary support vectors in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> with Equation <a href="#S3.E11" title="In Theorem 1 (DP guarantee of weight perturbation). ‣ 3.2 Weight Perturbation for All-in-one PMSVM ‣ 3 Differentially Private Multi-Class SVM ‣ Multi-Class Support Vector Machine with Differential Privacy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>, where <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{y,p}=e_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{y,p}=e_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>, thus <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\tilde{\mathbf{w}}}=2C/n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\tilde{\mathbf{w}}}=2C/n</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> norm with normalization to <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max(\|g(\cdot)\|_{2})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max(\|g(\cdot)\|_{2})=1</annotation></semantics></math>. This is a tightened version of <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta_{\tilde{\mathbf{w}}}=4C/n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta_{\tilde{\mathbf{w}}}=4C/n</annotation></semantics></math> in binary SVM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>.
Within all-in-one SVMs, we primarily focus on CS-SVM due to the ease of calculating <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\max}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\max}(G)</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e., <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{\lambda_{\max}(G)}=\sqrt{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{\lambda_{\max}(G)}=\sqrt{2}</annotation></semantics></math> due to <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu_{y,p}=e_{y}-e_{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ν</mi><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu_{y,p}=e_{y}-e_{p}</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore, we can expand the sensitivity of binary weight to multi-class weight at a cost of <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sqrt{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msqrt><mn>2</mn></msqrt><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sqrt{2}</annotation></semantics></math> ratio while reducing the access to training data regardless of the class numbers, which gives a significant advantage for the multi-class scenario, when <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&gt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&gt;2</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Gradient Perturbation for All-in-One PMSVM</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to solving the SVM dual solution through weight perturbation, we now focus on the primal solution and utilize a smoothed approximation of the hinge loss to compute gradients. Since gradient methods outperform output perturbation methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, we refer to our approach as gradient perturbation for PMSVM (PMSVM-GP). Specifically, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nie et al. [17]</a></cite> proposed a smoothed version of Equation <a href="#S2.E9" title="In Multi-Class Support Vector Machine ‣ 2.2 Multi-class Support Vector Machine ‣ 2 Backgrounds ‣ Multi-Class Support Vector Machine with Differential Privacy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> for gradient updates in all-in-one SVMs, introducing a small perturbation <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varsigma\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ς</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varsigma\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.E12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E12X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E12X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\min_{W,\mathbf{b}}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{k\neq y_{i}}\frac{\gamma_{ik}+\sqrt{\gamma_{ik}^{2}+\varsigma^{2}}}{2}+\frac{C}{n}\sum_{k&lt;l}\|{\mathbf{w}}_{k}-{\mathbf{w}}_{l}\|_{2}^{2}+\mu(||{W}||^{2}_{F}+||\mathbf{b}||_{2}^{2})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>min</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐛</mi></mrow></msub><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></msup><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>ς</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>C</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><mi>𝐰</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐰</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em">+</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>W</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" stretchy="false">|</mo><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">+</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>𝐛</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min_{W,\mathbf{b}}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{k\neq y_{i}}\frac{\gamma_{ik}+\sqrt{\gamma_{ik}^{2}+\varsigma^{2}}}{2}+\frac{C}{n}\sum_{k&lt;l}\|{\mathbf{w}}_{k}-{\mathbf{w}}_{l}\|_{2}^{2}+\mu(||{W}||^{2}_{F}+||\mathbf{b}||_{2}^{2}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{ik}=1-\bigl({\mathbf{w}}_{y_{i}}^{\!\top}x_{i}+b_{y_{i}}-{\mathbf{w}}_{k}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}_{i}-b_{k}\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{ik}=1-\bigl({\mathbf{w}}_{y_{i}}^{\!\top}x_{i}+b_{y_{i}}-{\mathbf{w}}_{k}^{\top}{\mathbf{x}}_{i}-b_{k}\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math>
is replaced by the smooth approximation
<math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{\varsigma}(\gamma_{ik})=\bigl(\gamma_{ik}+\sqrt{\gamma_{ik}^{2}+\varsigma^{2}}\bigr)\!\big/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>ς</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>ς</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.052em" maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true" symmetric="true">/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{\varsigma}(\gamma_{ik})=\bigl(\gamma_{ik}+\sqrt{\gamma_{ik}^{2}+\varsigma^{2}}\bigr)\!\big/2</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varsigma&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ς</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varsigma&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> is a small regularization parameter to ensure a unique solution.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmdefinition4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmdefinition4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4</span></span><span id="Thmdefinition4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Gradient Perturbation)<span id="Thmdefinition4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmdefinition4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmdefinition4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\mathbf{w}}}_{t+1}=\hat{{\mathbf{w}}}_{t}-\eta_{t}\hat{{\mathbf{g}}}_{t}=\hat{{\mathbf{w}}}_{t}-\eta_{t}[\mathcal{M}_{t}({\mathbf{w}}_{t},\mathcal{D})+{\mathbf{z}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐠</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>𝐳</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\mathbf{w}}}_{t+1}=\hat{{\mathbf{w}}}_{t}-\eta_{t}\hat{{\mathbf{g}}}_{t}=\hat{{\mathbf{w}}}_{t}-\eta_{t}[\mathcal{M}_{t}({\mathbf{w}}_{t},\mathcal{D})+{\mathbf{z}}]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmdefinition4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
where <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{z}}_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_{\mathbf{w}_{t}}^{2}\mathbf{I})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ</mi><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{z}}_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,\sigma_{\mathbf{w}_{t}}^{2}\mathbf{I})</annotation></semantics></math> for update step <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,\ldots,T-1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,\ldots,T-1]</annotation></semantics></math> with gradient update mechanism <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and learning rate <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. The final weight of the gradient update is <math id="Thmdefinition4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{w}}=\hat{\mathbf{w}}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{w}}=\hat{\mathbf{w}}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Due to the strong convexity of (<a href="#S3.E12" title="In 3.3 Gradient Perturbation for All-in-One PMSVM ‣ 3 Differentially Private Multi-Class SVM ‣ Multi-Class Support Vector Machine with Differential Privacy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>), following the proof of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> and the positive definiteness of its Hessian matrix, the convergence of the loss function with gradient methods is guaranteed.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemma2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlemma2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2</span></span><span id="Thmlemma2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlemma2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlemma2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Moments accountant <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>). There exist constant <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> so that given total steps <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> and sampling probability <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, for any <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&lt;c_{1}q^{2}T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&lt;c_{1}q^{2}T</annotation></semantics></math>, gradient updates guarantee (<math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon,\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon,\delta</annotation></semantics></math>)-DP, for any <math id="Thmlemma2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> if we choose</span></p>
<table id="S3.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma\geq c_{2}\frac{q\sqrt{T\log(1/\delta})}{\epsilon}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ϵ</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma\geq c_{2}\frac{q\sqrt{T\log(1/\delta})}{\epsilon}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">To ensure the gradient updates are private, we use differentially private gradient descent (DP-GD) or its mini-batch stochastic version, differentially private stochastic gradient descent (DP-SGD). For updates, we should choose the noise level <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> with the privacy budget <math id="S3.SS3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon,\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon,\delta)</annotation></semantics></math> as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(DP guarantee of gradient perturbation)<span id="Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\mathbf{w}}}_{t+1}=\hat{{\mathbf{w}}}_{t}-\eta_{t}[\mathcal{M}_{t}({\mathbf{w}}_{t},\mathcal{D})+{\mathbf{z}}_{t}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\mathbf{w}}}_{t+1}=\hat{{\mathbf{w}}}_{t}-\eta_{t}[\mathcal{M}_{t}({\mathbf{w}}_{t},\mathcal{D})+{\mathbf{z}}_{t}]</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> (Definition <a href="#Thmdefinition4" title="Definition 4 (Gradient Perturbation). ‣ 3.3 Gradient Perturbation for All-in-One PMSVM ‣ 3 Differentially Private Multi-Class SVM ‣ Multi-Class Support Vector Machine with Differential Privacy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) and its final weight <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbf{w}}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbf{w}}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfy <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon,\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon,\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>-DP when updating as follows:</span></p>
<table id="S3.E14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{{\mathbf{w}}}_{t+1}=\hat{{\mathbf{w}}}_{t}-\eta_{t}\hat{{\mathbf{g}}}_{t}=\hat{{\mathbf{w}}}_{t}-\eta_{t}\Bigl\{\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\frac{\nabla^{(t)}\!\bigl({\mathbf{x}}_{i}\bigr)}{\max\textbf{}\!\bigl(1,\|\nabla^{(t)}\!\bigl({\mathbf{x}}_{i}\bigr)\|_{2}/R\bigr)}+{\mathbf{z}}_{t}\Bigr\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐠</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mfrac><mrow><msup><mo>∇</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo lspace="0em">⁡</mo><mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.222em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msup><mo>∇</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{{\mathbf{w}}}_{t+1}=\hat{{\mathbf{w}}}_{t}-\eta_{t}\hat{{\mathbf{g}}}_{t}=\hat{{\mathbf{w}}}_{t}-\eta_{t}\Bigl\{\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\frac{\nabla^{(t)}\!\bigl({\mathbf{x}}_{i}\bigr)}{\max\textbf{}\!\bigl(1,\|\nabla^{(t)}\!\bigl({\mathbf{x}}_{i}\bigr)\|_{2}/R\bigr)}+{\mathbf{z}}_{t}\Bigr\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where individual gradients of <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{x}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{x}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla^{(t)}\bigl({\mathbf{x}}_{i}\bigr):=\bigl[\nabla^{(t)}_{1}({\mathbf{x}}_{i}),\dots,\nabla^{(t)}_{c}({\mathbf{x}}_{i})\bigr]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mo>∇</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∇</mo><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∇</mo><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla^{(t)}\bigl({\mathbf{x}}_{i}\bigr):=\bigl[\nabla^{(t)}_{1}({\mathbf{x}}_{i}),\dots,\nabla^{(t)}_{c}({\mathbf{x}}_{i})\bigr]</annotation></semantics></math>,
are calculated as:</span></p>
<table id="S3.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{k}^{(t)}=\begin{cases}-\displaystyle\sum_{l\neq k}\frac{\gamma_{il}+\sqrt{\gamma_{il}^{2}+\varsigma^{2}}}{2\sqrt{\gamma_{il}^{2}+\varsigma^{2}}}\,\mathbf{x}_{i}+2\lambda\displaystyle\sum_{l\neq k}(\mathbf{w}_{k}-\mathbf{w}_{l})+2\mu{\mathbf{w}}_{k},&amp;k=y_{i},\\[12.0pt]
\displaystyle\frac{\gamma_{ik}+\sqrt{\gamma_{ik}^{2}+\varsigma^{2}}}{2\sqrt{\gamma_{ik}^{2}+\varsigma^{2}}}\,\mathbf{x}_{i}+2\lambda\displaystyle\sum_{l\neq k}(\mathbf{w}_{k}-\mathbf{w}_{l})+2\mu{\mathbf{w}}_{k},&amp;k\neq y_{i},\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∇</mo><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">−</mo><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>ς</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>ς</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>λ</mi><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐰</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐰</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>μ</mi><mi>𝐰</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msqrt><mrow><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>ς</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><msubsup><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>ς</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{k}^{(t)}=\begin{cases}-\displaystyle\sum_{l\neq k}\frac{\gamma_{il}+\sqrt{\gamma_{il}^{2}+\varsigma^{2}}}{2\sqrt{\gamma_{il}^{2}+\varsigma^{2}}}\,\mathbf{x}_{i}+2\lambda\displaystyle\sum_{l\neq k}(\mathbf{w}_{k}-\mathbf{w}_{l})+2\mu{\mathbf{w}}_{k},&amp;k=y_{i},\\[12.0pt]
\displaystyle\frac{\gamma_{ik}+\sqrt{\gamma_{ik}^{2}+\varsigma^{2}}}{2\sqrt{\gamma_{ik}^{2}+\varsigma^{2}}}\,\mathbf{x}_{i}+2\lambda\displaystyle\sum_{l\neq k}(\mathbf{w}_{k}-\mathbf{w}_{l})+2\mu{\mathbf{w}}_{k},&amp;k\neq y_{i},\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{z}}_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}\!\bigl(0,R^{2}\sigma^{2}\mathbf{I}\bigr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.798em;" width="0.798em"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐈</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{z}}_{t}\sim\mathcal{N}\!\bigl(0,R^{2}\sigma^{2}\mathbf{I}\bigr)</annotation></semantics></math> with the <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> in Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma2" title="Lemma 2. ‣ 3.3 Gradient Perturbation for All-in-One PMSVM ‣ 3 Differentially Private Multi-Class SVM ‣ Multi-Class Support Vector Machine with Differential Privacy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and individual gradient clipped to size <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Refer to the appendix of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> for the proof of the moments accountant of DP gradient methods, while we calculate the gradients following Equation <a href="#S3.E15" title="In Theorem 2 (DP guarantee of gradient perturbation). ‣ 3.3 Gradient Perturbation for All-in-One PMSVM ‣ 3 Differentially Private Multi-Class SVM ‣ Multi-Class Support Vector Machine with Differential Privacy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>.
To show the advantage of the proposed method by reducing the noise level, we now investigate the utility gain of our method compared to previous DP-SVMs in the same privacy budget. As the objective function is strictly convex, we can guarantee a tight error bound <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> with gradient updates as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmlemma3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmlemma3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3</span></span><span id="Thmlemma3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmlemma3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmlemma3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>)
Suppose <math id="Thmlemma3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(w)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(w)</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="Thmlemma3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>-strongly convex and let
<math id="Thmlemma3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbf{w}}=\arg\min_{{\mathbf{w}}}F({\mathbf{w}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><msub><mi>min</mi><mi>𝐰</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐰</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbf{w}}=\arg\min_{{\mathbf{w}}}F({\mathbf{w}})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Consider the stochastic gradient update</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{{\mathbf{w}}}_{t+1}={{\mathbf{w}}}_{t}-\eta_{t}[\mathcal{M}_{t}({\mathbf{w}}_{t},\mathcal{D})]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{{\mathbf{w}}}_{t+1}={{\mathbf{w}}}_{t}-\eta_{t}[\mathcal{M}_{t}({\mathbf{w}}_{t},\mathcal{D})]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmlemma3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmlemma3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where
<math id="Thmlemma3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\,\mathcal{M}_{t}({\mathbf{w}}_{t}),\mathcal{D}]=\nabla F({\mathbf{w}}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\,\mathcal{M}_{t}({\mathbf{w}}_{t}),\mathcal{D}]=\nabla F({\mathbf{w}}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="Thmlemma3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\|\mathcal{M}_{t}({\mathbf{w}}_{t})\|_{2}^{2}]\leq G^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\|\mathcal{M}_{t}({\mathbf{w}}_{t})\|_{2}^{2}]\leq G^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the learning rate schedule is
<math id="Thmlemma3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{t}=\tfrac{1}{\lambda\,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{t}=\tfrac{1}{\lambda\,t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, for any <math id="Thmlemma3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}\!\bigl[F({\mathbf{w}}_{T})-F(\tilde{\mathbf{w}})\bigr]=\mathcal{O}\!\Bigl(\tfrac{G^{2}\log(T)}{\lambda\,T}\Bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.497em;" width="0.497em"><mi>𝔼</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.654em;" width="0.654em"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\!\bigl[F({\mathbf{w}}_{T})-F(\tilde{\mathbf{w}})\bigr]=\mathcal{O}\!\Bigl(\tfrac{G^{2}\log(T)}{\lambda\,T}\Bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Then, by <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> is the ratio of <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> in the Gaussian noise of ours to that of the OvR and OvO settings, i.e., <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=\sigma_{\mathrm{ours}}/\sigma_{\mathrm{OvR}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>ours</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>OvR</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=\sigma_{\mathrm{ours}}/\sigma_{\mathrm{OvR}}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=\sigma_{\mathrm{ours}}/\sigma_{\mathrm{OvO}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>ours</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>OvO</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=\sigma_{\mathrm{ours}}/\sigma_{\mathrm{OvO}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can make a tighter bound of convergences of the noised gradient update <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{w}}_{T}^{(\tau)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{w}}_{T}^{(\tau)}</annotation></semantics></math> compared to non-private gradient update <math id="S3.SS3.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{w}}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{w}}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Utility Advantage)<span id="Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let each single–example loss <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(w,z_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(w,z_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> be <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>–Lipschitz and
the population objective <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F(w)\!=\!\mathbb{E}_{z}[f(w,z)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.108em" rspace="0.108em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F(w)\!=\!\mathbb{E}_{z}[f(w,z)]</annotation></semantics></math> be
<math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math>–strongly convex.
Consider the noisy gradient update</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="{\mathbf{w}}_{t+1}=\hat{{\mathbf{w}}}_{t}-\eta_{t}\hat{{\mathbf{g}}}_{t}={\mathbf{w}}_{t}-\eta_{t}\Bigl(n\nabla f({\mathbf{w}}_{t},\mathcal{D})+{\mathbf{z}}_{\tau}\Bigr),\qquad{\mathbf{z}}_{\tau}\sim\mathcal{N}\!\bigl(0,\tau^{2}\sigma^{2}\mathbf{I}\bigr)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐠</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mpadded style="width:0.798em;" width="0.798em"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>𝐈</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{w}}_{t+1}=\hat{{\mathbf{w}}}_{t}-\eta_{t}\hat{{\mathbf{g}}}_{t}={\mathbf{w}}_{t}-\eta_{t}\Bigl(n\nabla f({\mathbf{w}}_{t},\mathcal{D})+{\mathbf{z}}_{\tau}\Bigr),\qquad{\mathbf{z}}_{\tau}\sim\mathcal{N}\!\bigl(0,\tau^{2}\sigma^{2}\mathbf{I}\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> is the ratio of <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> in the Gaussian noise. Let <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{w}}_{T}^{(\tau)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{w}}_{T}^{(\tau)}</annotation></semantics></math> be the <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmtheorem3.p1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>th iteration with noise
<math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{z}}_{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>τ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{z}}_{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{w}}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{w}}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is without noise addition.
Then, with a decayed learning rate
<math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta(t)=\frac{1}{\lambda t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta(t)=\frac{1}{\lambda t}</annotation></semantics></math>,
for any <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\!&gt;\!1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\!&gt;\!1</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}\!\bigl[F({\mathbf{w}}_{T})-F\!\bigl({\mathbf{w}}_{T}^{(\tau)}\bigr)\bigr]=\mathcal{O}\!\Bigl(\tfrac{d\sigma^{2}\bigl(1-\tau^{2}\bigr)\log T}{\lambda\,T}\Bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.497em;" width="0.497em"><mi>𝔼</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.612em;" width="0.612em"><mi>F</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.654em;" width="0.654em"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.710em" minsize="1.710em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.710em" minsize="1.710em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\!\bigl[F({\mathbf{w}}_{T})-F\!\bigl({\mathbf{w}}_{T}^{(\tau)}\bigr)\bigr]=\mathcal{O}\!\Bigl(\tfrac{d\sigma^{2}\bigl(1-\tau^{2}\bigr)\log T}{\lambda\,T}\Bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem3.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem3.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Instead, with a constant learning rate <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta(t)=\eta=\frac{c}{\lambda}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>η</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>c</mi><mi>λ</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta(t)=\eta=\frac{c}{\lambda}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;c\leq\frac{1}{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;c\leq\frac{1}{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, for any <math id="Thmtheorem3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\!&gt;\!1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.108em">&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\!&gt;\!1</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}\!\bigl[F({\mathbf{w}}_{T})-F\!\bigl({\mathbf{w}}_{T}^{(\tau)}\bigr)\bigr]=\mathcal{O}\!\Bigl(c\,d\sigma^{2}\bigl(1-\tau^{2}\bigr)\Bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.497em;" width="0.497em"><mi>𝔼</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.612em;" width="0.612em"><mi>F</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mi>T</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>τ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.654em;" width="0.654em"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>τ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\!\bigl[F({\mathbf{w}}_{T})-F\!\bigl({\mathbf{w}}_{T}^{(\tau)}\bigr)\bigr]=\mathcal{O}\!\Bigl(c\,d\sigma^{2}\bigl(1-\tau^{2}\bigr)\Bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p8.1" class="ltx_p">Thus, we theoretically prove that the reduced noise level in the all-in-one classifier results in smaller error compared to non-private updated points.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p9.2" class="ltx_p">The main reason to use clipping<span id="S3.SS3.p9.2.1" class="ltx_text">-</span>based gradient updates in DP<span id="S3.SS3.p9.2.2" class="ltx_text">-</span>SGD is that these approaches are extensively studied, allowing us to leverage recent analytical techniques for gradient<span id="S3.SS3.p9.2.3" class="ltx_text">-</span>based optimization. By the moments accountant, we can lower the privacy cost of the composition to <math id="S3.SS3.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(O(q\epsilon\sqrt{T}),\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mi>T</mi></msqrt></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(O(q\epsilon\sqrt{T}),\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>-DP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>. Also, we can utilize the Poisson sub-sampling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite> for mini-batch update, since private optimization has limited access to the data samples due to the privacy-utility trade-offs.
Furthermore, adopting advanced techniques is feasible within our framework. For example, for stable convergence, we can adapt adaptive moment methods, such as Adam <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> and its DP variants <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>, update the weight of Equation <a href="#S3.E14" title="In Theorem 2 (DP guarantee of gradient perturbation). ‣ 3.3 Gradient Perturbation for All-in-One PMSVM ‣ 3 Differentially Private Multi-Class SVM ‣ Multi-Class Support Vector Machine with Differential Privacy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a> into adaptive gradient perturbation (AGP) as follows:</p>
<table id="S3.E16" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="S3.E16X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E16X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mphantom></mphantom><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E16X.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{\mathbf{m}}_{t}=\beta_{1}\hat{\mathbf{m}}_{t-1}+(1-\beta_{1})\,\hat{\mathbf{g}}_{t},\ \hat{\mathbf{v}}_{t}=\beta_{2}\hat{\mathbf{v}}_{t-1}+(1-\beta_{2})(\hat{\mathbf{g}}_{t}\odot\hat{\mathbf{g}}_{t})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>β</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐠</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>β</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐠</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐠</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{\mathbf{m}}_{t}=\beta_{1}\hat{\mathbf{m}}_{t-1}+(1-\beta_{1})\,\hat{\mathbf{g}}_{t},\ \hat{\mathbf{v}}_{t}=\beta_{2}\hat{\mathbf{v}}_{t-1}+(1-\beta_{2})(\hat{\mathbf{g}}_{t}\odot\hat{\mathbf{g}}_{t}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="2" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.E16Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E16Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{\mathbf{m}}_{t}=\frac{\hat{\mathbf{m}}_{t}}{1-\beta_{1}^{t}},\ \hat{\mathbf{v}}_{t}=\frac{\hat{\mathbf{v}}_{t}}{1-\beta_{2}^{t}},\ \hat{\mathbf{w}}_{t}=\hat{\mathbf{w}}_{t-1}-\eta\frac{\hat{\mathbf{m}}_{t}}{\sqrt{\hat{\mathbf{v}}_{t}}+\gamma}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>β</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi></msubsup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>η</mi><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mrow><msqrt><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{\mathbf{m}}_{t}=\frac{\hat{\mathbf{m}}_{t}}{1-\beta_{1}^{t}},\ \hat{\mathbf{v}}_{t}=\frac{\hat{\mathbf{v}}_{t}}{1-\beta_{2}^{t}},\ \hat{\mathbf{w}}_{t}=\hat{\mathbf{w}}_{t-1}-\eta\frac{\hat{\mathbf{m}}_{t}}{\sqrt{\hat{\mathbf{v}}_{t}}+\gamma},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p10.1" class="ltx_p">with the gradient momentum <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{m}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐦</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{m}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and the second-moment accumulator <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{v}}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐯</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{v}}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> of Equation <a href="#S3.E15" title="In Theorem 2 (DP guarantee of gradient perturbation). ‣ 3.3 Gradient Perturbation for All-in-One PMSVM ‣ 3 Differentially Private Multi-Class SVM ‣ Multi-Class Support Vector Machine with Differential Privacy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a> as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>, which helps to reduce the iteration complexity.
Because of the post-processing in Remark <a href="#Thmremark2" title="Remark 2. ‣ 2.1 Differential Privacy ‣ 2 Backgrounds ‣ Multi-Class Support Vector Machine with Differential Privacy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, Equation <a href="#S3.E16" title="In 3.3 Gradient Perturbation for All-in-One PMSVM ‣ 3 Differentially Private Multi-Class SVM ‣ Multi-Class Support Vector Machine with Differential Privacy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a> still guarantees <math id="S3.SS3.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\epsilon,\delta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\epsilon,\delta)</annotation></semantics></math>-DP guarantee of Lemma <a href="#Thmlemma2" title="Lemma 2. ‣ 3.3 Gradient Perturbation for All-in-One PMSVM ‣ 3 Differentially Private Multi-Class SVM ‣ Multi-Class Support Vector Machine with Differential Privacy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Related Works</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Existing DP-SVM methods primarily focus on binary classification tasks. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chaudhuri et al. [3]</a></cite> investigated the use of DP convex optimization in SVMs, and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rubinstein et al. [11]</a></cite> expanded the methods with kernels with weight perturbation in binary setups. As the support vectors of dual formulation are coupled with the subset of training data, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jain and Thakurta [12]</a></cite> published the private weight based on the interactive scenario of model users. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ding et al. [9]</a></cite> used the gradient method for SVM with smoothed loss. None of the following works on DP-SVMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> investigated the privacy amplification of multi-class SVMs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Park et al. [13]</a></cite> argued a similar research question to our paper that multi-class SVMs need multiple accesses for training. Rather than mitigating within the boundary of SVMs, they detoured from the method with a kernel clustering and labeling method. On the other hand, we directly utilized the all-in-one SVMs to reduce the number of data accesses, which is compatible with the non-DP methods, such as CS-SVM or M<sup id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_sup">3</sup>-SVM.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experiments</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Experimental Design</h3>

<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Datasets</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We used multi-class classification datasets from the University of California at Irvine (UCI) repository <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>]</cite> for various data types: Cornell (CS web pages), Dermatology (clinical skin records), HHAR (wearable activity sensors), ISOLET (spoken alphabet), USPS (hand-written digits), and Vehicle (vehicle silhouettes).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Baselines</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For comparison methods, we compared with both existing weight and gradient perturbation methods in DP-SVMs based on OVR strategies. For weight perturbation, we compared with PrivateSVM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> and OPERA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>. For gradient methods, we compare with GRPUA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>. Additionally, for gradient descent <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite> for a neural network classification, we used a linear layer (Linear), with the cross-entropy loss, which shares the same architecture but with the loss used in neural network classification.
We exclude the DP-SVM models having interaction with users <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> and local DP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Experimental details</h4>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For privacy budget, we fixed <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta=10^{-5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta=10^{-5}</annotation></semantics></math> on various <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math>. We reported the mean and standard deviation on each setting, where we used 20 runs for weight perturbation and 5 runs for gradient perturbations. We performed a grid search on each method to find the well-performing one on <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon=4</annotation></semantics></math>, and used the obtained parameters for each model on other epsilons. We searched on <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C/n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C/n</annotation></semantics></math> for weight perturbation, and learning rate <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>η</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, gradient steps <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>, and fixed the clipping <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=1</annotation></semantics></math> for gradient methods.
We further utilize the min-max scaler for weight perturbation to bound the input sensitivity to 1 and thus calculate the sensitivity of <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbf{w}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbf{w}}</annotation></semantics></math> easily. We utilize a Poisson sub-sampling batch size of 128 for gradient methods.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We utilized the SVM packages in Sklearn <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite> for weight perturbation, and the Opacus <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> for gradient descent methods based on Pytorch <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>.
All experiments were run on an Intel(R) Xeon(R) CPU E5-2680 v3 @ 2.50GHz and a single NVIDIA GeForce RTX 4090.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Code is available at <a href="https://github.com/JinseongP/private_multiclass_svm" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://github.com/JinseongP/private_multiclass_svm</a>.
Refer to Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Experiments ‣ Multi-Class Support Vector Machine with Differential Privacy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a> for further details of datasets and experimental settings.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T2" class="ltx_table">
