</figure>
<div id="A3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We present additional results on the ablation studies of learning rate shown in Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Learning rate decay ‣ 5.3 Additional Experiments ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Multi-Class Support Vector Machine with Differential Privacy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> for the remaining datasets in Table <a href="#A3.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ C.2 Additional Experiments ‣ Appendix C Experiments ‣ Multi-Class Support Vector Machine with Differential Privacy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>.
Furthermore, Table <a href="#A3.T10" title="Table 10 ‣ C.2 Additional Experiments ‣ Appendix C Experiments ‣ Multi-Class Support Vector Machine with Differential Privacy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> shows the difference between selecting <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>. It is true that there is no universal rule to choose <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math>, we concluded that <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=1</annotation></semantics></math> from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite> is a reasonable choice for the gradient method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>.
Table <a href="#A3.T11" title="Table 11 ‣ C.2 Additional Experiments ‣ Appendix C Experiments ‣ Multi-Class Support Vector Machine with Differential Privacy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> shows the results of different batch sizes for the Poisson subsampling in Opacus <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite>. We use <math id="A3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{min}(\text{batch size},\text{\# of training data})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>min</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>batch size</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext># of training data</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{min}(\text{batch size},\text{\# of training data})</annotation></semantics></math>. Compared to full batch gradients, the results of subsampling show better performance.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.T9" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 9: </span>Ablation study on the effect of learning rate decay for the proposed gradient perturbation methods. We bold the better performance in <span id="A3.T9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">bold</span> and Diff indicates the absolute difference w/ and w/o lr decay.</figcaption>
